  "protocolVersion": null,
  "instructions": "MobileScreen designs App Store / Google Play screenshot sets. This server gives you every control of the MobileScreen web editor, plus a renderer so you can see each change.\n\nWorkflow\n1. whoami (or login) -> list_projects / create_project.\n2. generate_screenshots with the app's raw screenshots (local PNG/JPEG files, e.g. from the simulator). It writes the headlines, picks a style and lays out every device and language. Costs 1 credit per screenshot per device. Pass what you know about the app from its code in app_description and direction.\n3. get_project shows every screen with its text layers, device placement and background. Screen ids look like \"iphone_0\"; they stay the same when screens are reordered.\n4. Edit with edit_texts, add_text, remove_text, edit_devices, set_backgrounds, reorder_screens, add_screen, delete_screen, set_screenshot, add_illustration, set_floating_image.\n5. After edits, look at the result: render_screens (single screens) or preview_set (the whole set side by side). Iterate until it looks right.\n6. export_screenshots writes the final PNGs (e.g. into the project's fastlane folder). Every change is saved to the project and shows up in the web editor (https://mobilescreen.co) after a reload.\n\nCoordinates (same as the editor)\n- Text layer location: y = top of the text box in % of screen height; width = box width in % of screen width (text wraps inside); alignment = left | center | right (left/right keep a 6% side margin).\n- font.size = px on a 320px-wide screen (16 = 5% of the screen width). font.letterSpacing is in em (-0.03 = tight). font.lineHeight is a multiple of the size. font.weight 100-900, font.style normal | italic, font.color hex.\n- runs style parts of a text: [{text, color?, family?, weight?, style?, sizeScale?, letterSpacing?}] (e.g. the payoff word in the accent color). Their texts joined must be the whole text; \"\\n\" is a line break.\n- Text is per language; font and location are shared by all languages.\n- Chinese, Japanese and Thai have no spaces to wrap at: put \\n where each line should break, and check the render.\n- Device: offsetX / offsetY in % of screen width / height (-100..100) from the generated placement, scale in % (10..300, 100 = generated size), rotation in degrees (-180..180). A device that overhangs its screen shows on the neighbouring screen, like on the store, so sets can flow across screens.\n- Backgrounds belong to a screen index and are shared by every device (iphone_2 and ipad_2 have the same background).\n\nDesign bar\nAim for the 2026 top charts: one short, specific headline per screen (2 lines at most) that names a real benefit in the product's own words, one highlighted payoff phrase, generous whitespace, consistent type across the set, the device large and legible. Avoid filler like \"experience\", \"seamless\", \"unlock\", \"all-in-one\".",
  "durationMs": 7336,
  "stderr": ""
